WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:07.790
On July 5th, the White House posted this seemingly
uncontroversial and unremarkable Tweet —

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:11.130
a list of places it had donated vaccines.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:16.630
It seemed like a banal government press release
few would give much thought to.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:22.930
That is, until it was suddenly deleted — and
for fear of causing an international incident,

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:24.550
no less.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.670
See if you can find why.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:31.400
The problem was not the actual donation.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:36.320
Nor was it the mere reference to “Taiwan”
— a word the U.S. government uses all the

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.320
time.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.230
And nowhere in the Tweet were the words “country”
or “nation”.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:47.300
No, the concern was about this tiny flag,
which the White House called an “honest

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.300
mistake”.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:54.220
So fragile is this issue that merely tweeting
an icon of a flag might be interpreted as

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:59.510
a change in policy, or even a recognition
of statehood — something that could trigger

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.090
a military response by China.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.370
And yet so too could deleting the Tweet cause
confusion.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:12.850
Afterward, the Taiwanese Foreign Ministry
asked the U.S. not to “cause unnecessary

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:18.030
speculation” after its president and de
facto ambassador were left retweeting a message

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.320
now deleted.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.549
This was not the first such diplomatic mishap,
nor, surely, will it be the last.

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:31.860
The U.S. government practices a strategy of
deliberate ambiguity, leading much of the

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:37.970
public, many in the media, and even a few
politicians to misunderstand its position.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:43.030
Despite what China would have you believe,
the U.S. does not, in fact, adhere to the

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.159
“One-China” Principle.

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:49.920
Neither does it support Taiwanese independence,
though it does have “unofficial” relations

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.750
with its government.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.920
The reality is much more complicated...

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.400
Sponsored by Morning Brew — the free, daily
newsletter I read to stay up to date on the

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.360
world of tech and business.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.560
When Mao Zedong founded the People’s Republic
of China in 1949 and promised to quote “liberate”

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.379
Taiwan, the U.S. initially decided not to
intervene.

00:02:14.379 --> 00:02:19.849
Only after the invasion of South Korea in
the summer of 1950 did the Truman administration

00:02:19.849 --> 00:02:24.220
begin recognizing the strategic value of Taiwan.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:30.200
Four years later, in 1954, the U.S. signed
a defense treaty, promising to protect Taiwan

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:31.440
if attacked.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:39.309
This, along with America’s refusal to recognize
the PRC, lasted for another 25 years, until

00:02:39.309 --> 00:02:43.180
it discovered a bigger threat: The Soviet
Union.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:48.719
Largely in response to the Communists up North,
America finally recognized the People’s

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:54.290
Republic in 1979 as the, quote, “sole legal
Government of China.”

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:59.639
It promised not to pursue a “two Chinas”
or “One China, one Taiwan” solution, which

00:02:59.639 --> 00:03:03.010
meant ending official relations with Taiwan.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.519
It also stated, in a communiqué, “...the
United States of America acknowledges the

00:03:07.519 --> 00:03:12.209
Chinese position that there is but one China
and Taiwan is part of China”

00:03:12.209 --> 00:03:17.469
Upon hearing this, you, like many people,
might conclude that the U.S. acquiesced to

00:03:17.469 --> 00:03:19.319
the People’s Republic.

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:22.670
But if so, you’re not paying close enough
attention.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:28.069
It recognizes the PRC as the “sole legal
government of China” but doesn’t define

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:30.500
what China is.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:35.129
Remember, Taiwan is formally known as the
“Republic of China”, and both claimed

00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:37.079
to be “China”.

00:03:37.079 --> 00:03:41.439
On the other hand, it merely acknowledges
“...the Chinese position that there is but

00:03:41.439 --> 00:03:44.370
one China and Taiwan is part of China”.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:49.069
The key word here being “acknowledge”
— which is not the same as “accept”

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:50.829
or “recognize”.

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:56.239
It does, however, give a similar impression
— at least on a cursory reading.

00:03:56.239 --> 00:03:58.319
And this is certainly no accident.

00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:04.769
The American position then and now is to obfuscate
— to narrowly satisfy China’s stringent

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:09.319
demands while giving up as little as possible
in the process.

00:04:09.319 --> 00:04:15.049
The U.S. could argue it fulfilled its obligations,
while China could argue the U.S. fully agreed

00:04:15.049 --> 00:04:16.160
to its position.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.470
A mutually enforced facade of resolution.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:26.340
In reality, nothing said or done back in ‘79
truly resolved the issue.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.720
Both sides only kicked the can further down
the road.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.870
And far worse than simply delaying that ugly
conversation, the talks created the superficial

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:40.180
illusion of agreement, leading to hurt feelings
and accusations later on.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:46.850
In other words: America spoke as vaguely as
possible, which China gladly exploited.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:51.420
The Chinese translation of the document, for
example, uses a word closer in meaning to

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:56.280
“recognize”, despite there being an accurate
translation of “acknowledge” available.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:01.770
It’s quite possible that many in China and
even Taiwan, most of whom read the document

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:06.290
in Chinese, believe the U.S. promised more
than it actually did.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:09.830
That was, after all, kind of the point.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:14.110
There’s another, similar linguistic misconception.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:18.640
The “One-China Principle” is the term
for China’s position — that Taiwan is

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.080
a part of the People’s Republic.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:26.850
This is not the same as generic, lower-case
“One-China policies” — which every country

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:29.090
has a different version of.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:33.730
When China accuses another country of violating
the “One-China Principle”, it’s really

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:40.300
only saying that it disagrees with the action,
not that the other country has broken a promise.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:46.570
Make no mistake: The U.S. does not adhere
to the Principle, only to its own policy — one

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:51.080
which has gone unchanged for over forty years.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:58.120
the People’s Republic, the U.S. ended its
defense treaty with Taiwan, replacing it instead

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.840
with the Taiwan Relations Act.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:05.780
The TRA was designed to reassume Taiwan by
providing it with the weapons it needs to

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:07.010
defend itself.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:11.850
And while it states an expectation that the
issue will be resolved peacefully, it does

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:16.930
not guarantee military support, only that
the President will inform Congress of any

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:20.500
potential threat, and then take “appropriate
action”.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:26.140
Still, all parties operated under the assumption
of eventual unification.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:32.290
All that changed in the 90s, as Taiwan transitioned
from brutal authoritarian rule to what is

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:37.190
today one of the strongest and most vibrant
democracies on earth.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:43.690
The process was neither smooth nor easy, and
made advocating for independence legal, complicating

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:45.330
the situation.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:51.030
Today the future status of Taiwan is still
the primary issue that divides parties.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:56.640
On one side are the “Blues”, who favor
closer economic ties to China.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.800
China buys nearly half of Taiwan’s exports,
which they see as a critical relationship

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.200
to maintain.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.850
To them, China is less an adversary and more
a business partner.

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:12.990
The dominant party in this camp is the Kuomintang,
or KMT.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:19.510
On the other side are the “Greens”, led
by the Democratic Progressive Party, DPP.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:25.230
Greens see trade with China as a dangerous
dependency that leads to economic coercion.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:29.080
They prefer the term “Taiwan” to “The
Republic of China”.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:36.010
But while the KMT is sometimes sloppily labeled
“pro-unification”, and the DPP “pro-independence”,

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.380
this is simply not true.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:43.540
In reality, both sides agree that maintaining
the status quo is the best short-to-medium

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:51.720
term strategy, and neither side, except for
a few radicals, advocates unification or independence.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:57.300
The Greens argue Taiwan is already an independent,
sovereign state, and thus has no reason to

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:01.730
declare independence — something which would
surely start a war.

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:06.780
None of this is meant to discount the ferocity
of Taiwanese politics — healthy democracy

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:12.060
is fierce and messy — but to highlight how
subtle their differences.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.030
This graph helps put things in perspective.

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:19.330
When asked their preferences, the Taiwanese
public has answered remarkably consistently

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:21.320
for twenty years.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:26.310
The top three responses are all “maintain
the status quo”, and only differ in the

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:27.970
long term.

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:35.269
28% prefer to “decide at a later date”,
27% wish to maintain the status quo indefinitely,

00:08:35.269 --> 00:08:40.169
and one quarter wish to “maintain the status
quo but move toward independence”.

00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:46.010
Only a small minority wish to unify immediately,
move toward unification, or declare independence

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:47.370
as soon as possible.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:53.279
Ironically, it’s most often Americans, halfway
across the globe, who panic when China flies

00:08:53.279 --> 00:08:58.290
fighter jets through Taiwanese airspace, not
the Taiwanese public, for whom such shows

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:01.560
of intimidation have become mundane.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:05.310
Most are content simply keeping things as
they are.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:07.019
As is the United States.

00:09:07.019 --> 00:09:10.749
Officially, it “does not support” independence.

00:09:10.749 --> 00:09:14.629
But remember: American diplomats are precise
in their meaning.

00:09:14.629 --> 00:09:19.810
The U.S. does not say it opposes independence,
which would mean taking a position.

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:24.949
But while it does not take a stance on the
outcome, it does advocate for the process.

00:09:24.949 --> 00:09:30.279
Not only does it oppose a forceful unification
by China, but so too does it oppose a unilateral

00:09:30.279 --> 00:09:36.500
declaration of independence by Taiwan — something
which would also destabilize the region.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:41.709
When Taiwan’s president proposed a public
referendum on independence in 2003, the U.S.

00:09:41.709 --> 00:09:44.670
was caught between a rock and a hard place.

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:49.730
On one hand, it did not support what it perceived
as dangerous antagonizing.

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:54.740
On the other, it did not want to publicly
oppose a legal, democratic process.

00:09:54.740 --> 00:10:01.270
Instead, it reiterated indirectly, but unequivocally,
that it did not support independence — sending

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:04.509
the message that the president had crossed
a line.

00:10:04.509 --> 00:10:08.819
Since then, Taiwanese officials have gone
out of their way to prove they don’t plan

00:10:08.819 --> 00:10:11.619
on rocking the boat.

00:10:11.619 --> 00:10:16.470
In 2018, current President Tsai Ing-Wen stayed
silent on a referendum calling for its Olympic

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:19.790
name to be changed from “Chinese-Taipei”
to “Taiwan”.

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:22.910
And she’s in the Green camp.

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:27.899
Every major politician today understands the
need to proceed with caution — repeating,

00:10:27.899 --> 00:10:32.149
sometimes monotonously, that they won’t
pursue independence.

00:10:32.149 --> 00:10:36.709
That doesn’t stop China from claiming they
support independence, however, which engenders

00:10:36.709 --> 00:10:41.540
Nationalism at home and helps narrow the boundaries
of what’s “acceptable”.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:45.089
But things can’t continue like this forever.

00:10:45.089 --> 00:10:50.999
While America and Taiwan support the status
quo for now, everyone agrees it’s untenable

00:10:50.999 --> 00:10:52.709
in the long term.

00:10:52.709 --> 00:10:58.089
One of three red lines China says the crossing
of which would trigger an attack, is if Taiwan

00:10:58.089 --> 00:11:02.290
refuses to ever consider negotiating unification.

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:07.680
The possibility of change — however minuscule
— is thus imperative to maintaining the

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.939
peaceful status quo.

00:11:09.939 --> 00:11:14.329
The question is: in what direction does time
favor?

00:11:14.329 --> 00:11:17.360
In terms of public opinion, the answer is
clear.

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:22.019
When broken down by age, it’s evident that
younger generations feel an increasingly strong

00:11:22.019 --> 00:11:25.360
Taiwanese identity distinct from China.

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:31.110
The 2014 Sunflower Movement demonstrated how
determined young people are, which recent

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:35.930
developments in Hong Kong will surely only
convince them of more so.

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:40.709
And yet, every day China’s military and
economy grow stronger.

00:11:40.709 --> 00:11:45.199
It may believe time is on its side — that
the balance of power will only shift in its

00:11:45.199 --> 00:11:46.199
favor.

00:11:46.199 --> 00:11:50.990
Likewise, does it surely notice America’s
growing disinterest in acting as the world’s

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:52.439
police force.

00:11:52.439 --> 00:11:56.881
China may doubt the will of Americans to fight
a war so distant at a time when it’s so

00:11:56.881 --> 00:11:59.430
preoccupied at home.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:04.389
In the meantime, Taiwan is stuck in a historically
unprecedented limbo.

00:12:04.389 --> 00:12:10.819
A weird in-between state in which it can enjoy
freedom only if it pretends not to,

00:12:10.819 --> 00:12:16.770
A status quo that has somehow survived eight
American administrations, forty years, and

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:23.199
yet can suddenly be called into question with
a mere icon of flag in a Tweet.

00:12:23.199 --> 00:12:26.879
